From: Cezary Wilmanski <cezary.wilmanski@adtran.com>
Date: Mon, 08 Sep 2025 22:59:05 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] net: phy: aquantia: remove polling GLOBAL_CFG reg

Previous patches introduced a workaround for an issue observed on AQR115C,
where after suspend the PHY firmware signaled readiness but the GLOBAL_CFG registers
continued to return zeros for some time. That commit added polling of
the 100M GLOBAL_CFG register until it returned a non-zero value, since AQR113,
AQR113C and AQR115C were known to support it.

However, on AQR113C, the polling still reads back zeros at this stage,
which only leads to a spurious error during configuration initialization.
This happens because at that point the PHY is not yet ready due to being held in reset by GPIO,
so the register cannot return valid values anyway.

As a result, the polling logic is dropped entirely.
This reverts the workaround and avoids unnecessary initialization errors.

Signed-off-by: Cezary Wilmanski <cezary.wilmanski@adtran.com>
---
 drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_main.c |   21 +--------------------
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 20 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/aquantia/aquantia_main.c
@@ -1034,21 +1034,7 @@ static int aqr_gen2_fill_interface_modes
 	unsigned long *possible = phydev->possible_interfaces;
 	struct aqr107_priv *priv = phydev->priv;
 	phy_interface_t interface;
-	int i, val, ret;
-
-	/* It's been observed on some models that - when coming out of suspend
-	 * - the FW signals that the PHY is ready but the GLOBAL_CFG registers
-	 * continue on returning zeroes for some time. Let's poll the 100M
-	 * register until it returns a real value as both 113c and 115c support
-	 * this mode.
-	 */
-	if (priv->wait_on_global_cfg) {
-		ret = phy_read_mmd_poll_timeout(phydev, MDIO_MMD_VEND1,
-						VEND1_GLOBAL_CFG_100M, val,
-						val != 0, 1000, 100000, false);
-		if (ret)
-			return ret;
-	}
+	int i, ret;
 
 	ret = aqr_gen2_read_global_syscfg(phydev);
 	if (ret)
